  "body": "On next, `b3.h` says `b3_hasher_finalize` leaves the hasher \"finished and holds no message; `b3_hasher_new` starts the next\". The implementation reads the Hasher out and leaves its bytes unchanged.\n\nRelease build, exact ddb7199d7f0b0f04390e923474b630ffc7ff2811 through the C API's own entry points (`safety-capi-reuse-epyc-20261108T151551-cecf68fe`, VERIFIED): new, update(\"abc\"), finalize, then\n\n- finalize again: **hash(\"abc\") again**;\n- update(\"xyz\") without new, finalize: **hash(\"abcxyz\")**, not hash(\"xyz\").\n\nA cursory C reader can take \"holds no message\" to mean \"empty and ready\", and get a silently wrong digest for every message after the first. Released main 6e0dffd3 had a `const` finalize, repeatable, as official BLAKE3's C API; code ported from either keeps updating after finalize and now relies on undocumented continuation. The memory accesses stay valid (Hasher owns nothing), so this is a wrong-result and contract-clarity issue, not memory unsafety. The keyed key and staged bytes also remain in caller memory after finalize, as before.\n\nCandidate remedies, your choice: write the fresh state for the same mode after the read (cheap: the core, not the 16 KiB stage), making the doc's sentence true; or reword b3.h to say any call other than `b3_hasher_new` on a finished hasher breaks the contract. A fail-stop check would need a state marker, which may not earn its place.\n\n\u2014 Sam Shackleton",
